[NativeAOT] Read the byte-packed LSDA offsets without a misaligned wide load

The unwind blob a method's LSDA points at is byte-packed: EmitLsda in
UnixObjectWriter.Aot.cs writes a one-byte flags field and then the 32-bit
main-LSDA, method-start, EH-info and associated-data offsets with no padding.
UnixNativeCodeManager and RuntimeInstance read those offsets back with
*dac_cast<PTR_int32_t>(p) - a 32-bit load from an address one byte past
whatever alignment the section gave the blob. It is misaligned by
construction, on every target.

x64 and arm64 do not care. RISC-V is not required to implement misaligned
access: on hardware without Zicclsm the load traps, and Linux then emulates
it in a kernel trap handler, which is correct but hundreds of cycles for a
load on what is an unwinding hot path. Where nothing emulates it - a
bare-metal or zkVM executor, or a runtime built -mstrict-align - it is
fatal, and the first site hit in practice is EHEnumInit.

Read the offsets a byte at a time instead, through the decoder that already
reads these very blobs. NativePrimitiveDecoder grows a non-mutating
PeekInt32, and the existing ReadInt32/ReadUInt32 are expressed in terms of
it rather than repeating the byte assembly a third time; that also drops the
signed overflow the old expressions had in the top byte. Decoding
little-endian byte by byte is what the writer asks for anyway - it emits the
field with WriteLittleEndian<int>.

Nothing changes for a target that implements misaligned access: the byte
loads fold back into one wide load. All four call sites still compile to a
single ldrsw on arm64 and a single movslq on x64, and on riscv64 to a single
lw unless the build asks for -mstrict-align.
--- a/src/coreclr/nativeaot/Runtime/NativePrimitiveDecoder.h
+++ b/src/coreclr/nativeaot/Runtime/NativePrimitiveDecoder.h
@@ -46,17 +46,31 @@ public:
         return value;
     }
 
+    // The encodings read here are byte-packed, so a 32-bit field can start at any
+    // address. Assemble it out of byte loads: a wide load at a misaligned address
+    // faults on a target that does not implement misaligned access, and a compiler
+    // for a target that does implement it folds these back into one wide load.
+    static uint32_t PeekUInt32(const uint8_t* p)
+    {
+        return (uint32_t)*p | ((uint32_t)*(p+1) << 8) | ((uint32_t)*(p+2) << 16) | ((uint32_t)*(p+3) << 24);
+    }
+
+    static int32_t PeekInt32(const uint8_t* p)
+    {
+        return (int32_t)PeekUInt32(p);
+    }
+
     static int32_t ReadInt32(uint8_t* & p)
     {
-        int32_t value = *p | (*(p+1) << 8) | (*(p+2) << 16) | (*(p+3) << 24);
-        p += 4;
+        int32_t value = PeekInt32(p);
+        p += sizeof(int32_t);
         return value;
     }
 
     static uint32_t ReadUInt32(uint8_t* & p)
     {
-        uint32_t value = *p | (*(p+1) << 8) | (*(p+2) << 16) | (*(p+3) << 24);
-        p += 4;
+        uint32_t value = PeekUInt32(p);
+        p += sizeof(uint32_t);
         return value;
     }
 };
